, raters unanimously call the race Safe or Likely Democratic, and markets price her near 95%. The Republican nominee's self-inflicted damage, including his sister's public endorsement of his opponent, reinforces a clear Democratic edge.", "watch": "A late affordability-focused Republican surge or vote-splitting with independent Rick Bennett could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Janet Mills (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Maine's governorship is open as term limits keep Janet Mills off the ballot, and Democrat Hannah Pingree enters the fall as a clear favorite. Polls from YouGov, SSRS, UNH, and Siena have consistently shown her leading Republican Bobby Charles by high single digits to low double digits, with the most recent surveys in early September around 9 to 13 points. Expert raters uniformly place the race in the Safe or Likely Democratic column, and prediction markets price a Pingree win near 95 percent.\n\nPingree has outraised Charles roughly two to one and holds a cash advantage, and national Democratic groups have scaled back spending out of confidence. The race has centered on affordability and gun policy, and Charles has drawn negative attention from his own sister's public endorsement of Pingree. Independent Rick Bennett's presence adds some uncertainty, but the overall evidence points to a comfortable Democratic hold.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9757, "margin": 10.9}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9716, "margin": 10.5}]}